{"name":"Moire Satin","hex":"#665d63","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"moire","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Originally, a fine textile fabric made of the hair of an Asiatic goat.","Any textile fabric to which a watered appearance is given."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moire"},{"term":"satin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cloth woven from silk, nylon or polyester with a glossy surface and a dull back. (The same weaving technique applied to cotton produces cloth termed sateen).","Gin (the drink)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Semigloss."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/satin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moiré pattern","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematics, physics, and art, a moiré pattern or moiré fringe is a large-scale wave interference pattern that can be produced when a partially opaque ruled pattern with transparent gaps is overlaid on another similar pattern. For the moiré interference pattern to appear, the two patterns must not be completely identical, but rather displaced, rotated, or have slightly different pitch."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Moir%C3%A9_pattern.svg/330px-Moir%C3%A9_pattern.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moir%C3%A9_pattern","wikidata":"Q26468"},{"title":"Satin","blocks":[{"text":"A satin weave is a type of fabric weave that produces a characteristically glossy, smooth or lustrous material, typically with a glossy top surface and a dull back; it is not durable, as it tends to snag. It is one of three fundamental types of textile weaves alongside plain weave and twill weave."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Satin_weave_in_silk_90.jpg/330px-Satin_weave_in_silk_90.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satin","wikidata":"Q1